## Code quality

The code is fit for a long-lived codebase shared with human developers. The
type-check, lint, and format checks pass, and unit tests cover the simulation.

## Coordinate system and presentation

All positions and sizes in this specification are given in the game's own
logical units on a fixed stage of `STAGE_W x STAGE_H` (`1280 x 720`, 16:9). The
origin `(0, 0)` is the top-left; `x` increases to the right and `y` increases
downward. The stage center is `(STAGE_CX, STAGE_CY)` (`640, 360`). Where the
board sits within the stage, and where each cell's center falls, are given in
`specs/board.md`.

- `STAGE_W x STAGE_H` is the game's logical design size. Fitting it to the
  browser window is the runtime's: the uniform scale that preserves the aspect
  ratio, the letterboxed centering, and the device pixel ratio. The complete
  stage is therefore on screen at every window size, on load and at any pixel
  density.
- The letterbox bars around the stage carry the stage's background color.
- Draw in logical units, and take the canvas element's own size from the runtime
  alone.
- Game logic operates in logical units, independent of the rendered scale. The
  pointer position the game is given is in those same units, so a hit test
  against a cell center needs no conversion.

Every rate in this specification is per second and every duration is in seconds,
integrated against the delta time the runtime hands each update.

## Visual design

The board is the lit thing on the stage. Choose the colors and the typography,
decide how a gem is drawn and how a cut is marked on one, and decide what sits
behind the board and how the board answers a clear.

Whatever look you choose, a player reads all of the following at a glance.

| What a player reads | Requirement |
| --- | --- |
| Gem kind | Each of the seven kinds carries one distinct hue, and each also carries a form, marking, or facet pattern of its own, so the seven are told apart by more than hue. |
| Strain | A gem's four strain states read as damage deepening from clean to flawed, and a flawed gem stands apart from every unflawed one on the board. |
| Cut | A `brilliant`, a `star`, and a `prism` each read as distinct from a plain gem and from one another. |
| Prism | A `prism` reads as carrying no kind, distinct at a glance from every gem that carries one. |
| Gem size | Every gem's drawn form fits inside `GEM_R` (`30`) of its cell center. |
| Selection and offer | The selected cell reads distinctly against a board of gems, and while an offer stands the two gems it names are drawn exchanged, so a player sees the move a release would play. |
| Motion | A swap, a shattering set, and falling gems are each drawn moving over the spans `specs/rules.md` gives them, and a `brilliant`, a `star`, and a `prism` each carry the running effect `specs/assets.md` produces for them. |
| Controls | Every pointer target `specs/controls.md` names is drawn where it is reported, large enough to be worked by a fingertip, so a player with only a touchscreen reaches every screen. |
| Readouts | The score, the level, the level meter, and the chain multiplier are legible at the logical stage size and sit clear of the board's extent, which `specs/board.md` gives. |

The produced art and sounds carry the rest of the look, and `specs/assets.md`
holds them to the same bar. The palette, type, glow, and every other aspect of
the look are yours.
